WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.590
Put twelve points equally spaced round a circle,

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:05.170
like the hours on a clock.

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:07.505
From the centre, the full turn of three hundred and

00:00:07.505 --> 00:00:10.980
sixty degrees is shared into twelve equal angles

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:12.130
of thirty degrees.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:15.640
Counting gaps between points tells you an angle

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:16.310
at the centre.

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:20.150
Clock problems, wheels and regular shapes all

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:21.080
use this idea.

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:24.550
And there is a second fact, about angles drawn from a

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:25.850
point on the circle itself,

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:28.240
that unlocks many harder problems.

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:31.850
On the clock circle, join the centre to the point at

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:33.840
twelve and to the point at four.

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:35.960
What angle is made at the centre?

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:37.600
Pause and count carefully.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:43.390
From twelve to four there are four gaps.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:46.590
Four times thirty is one hundred and twenty degrees.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:48.890
Count the gaps, not the points.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:51.910
There are five points from twelve to four,

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:53.430
but only four gaps.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:57.320
Now stand at a point on the circle instead, at eight,

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:00.290
and look at the same arc from twelve to four.

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:03.380
The angle you see is exactly half the angle at the

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:04.830
centre: sixty degrees.

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:08.440
An angle on the circle is half the angle at the centre

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:10.070
standing on the same arc.

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:12.080
Why half here?

00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:13.720
Draw the radius to eight.

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:17.760
It splits the angle at eight into two parts, a and b.

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:21.710
Since the radii are equal, the two small triangles have

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:22.820
equal base angles.

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:25.330
Their angles at the centre are one hundred and eighty

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:29.380
minus two a, and one hundred and eighty minus two b.

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:31.600
Subtract both from a full turn.

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:35.590
The centre angle over the arc is two a plus two b,

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:37.660
twice the whole angle at eight.

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:40.360
A special case is worth remembering.

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:44.410
If the arc is exactly half the circle, the angle at the

00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:47.410
centre is one hundred and eighty, so the angle on the

00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:48.400
circle is ninety.

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:52.310
Any triangle drawn with a diameter as one side,

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:55.820
and its third corner on the circle, has a right angle.

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:59.010
Share three hundred and sixty among the gaps.

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:01.030
Count gaps, not points.

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:04.430
The angle on the circle is half the angle at the centre.

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:08.380
Notebook challenge: on the clock circle, what is the

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:11.890
angle at the centre between the points at two and seven?
